The movement system is shared: one system, filtered to both ClientSimulation and ServerSimulation. Someone profiles the client and notices the friction calculation is the expensive part of a system that runs several times per frame during replay. Since the server is authoritative anyway, they gate it:

    if (isServer) ApplyFriction(ref velocity, surface);

The client is cheaper. Movement still feels identical — friction is subtle. Playtests are fine on a LAN.

Pickups. Server-authoritative, but you want the score number on the HUD to pop the instant the player touches one, so the increment lives in a predicted system:

    if (Overlaps(player, pickup)) { score.Value += 1; ecb.DestroyEntity(pickup); }

The same system, same filter, runs on the server too. In testing the score is correct. In a playtest with 4% loss, players report the score "counting up on its own" for a moment and then correcting.
